---
import Section from "./Section.astro";
---

<Section id="what" title="What it sends, and what it never does">
  <div class="grid gap-x-8 md:grid-cols-2 [&_h3]:mt-0 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-5">
    <div>
      <h3>It sends</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>To <a href="https://hearthtale.app">hearthtale.app</a></strong>: each character's name, realm, race and
          class, the journal Hearthtale wrote at logout, and the link code if you typed one with /ht link. The addon's raw
          records stay on your computer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>To <a href="https://wow-locker.app">wow-locker.app</a></strong>: the WoWLocker addon's saved file, as
          it is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>When you sign in with Battle.net</strong>: your Battle.net id, your BattleTag and the characters on
          the account, to hearthtale.app and, if you linked it, wow-locker.app. That's how they know which characters
          are yours.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>To the addons' shared game database, only if you say yes</strong>: with "Share what you see" ticked in
          Hearthtale, Lorekeeper's Codex, Yonder or Second Wind, and Ravenpost linked with Battle.net, what the game
          showed you of the world. Quest texts, creatures, what characters say, what a creature dropped. It helps the
          addons cover World of Warcraft: Forever's new content sooner. The Battle.net link counts you as one voice when
          players' games disagree.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Once a day</strong>, to its own inbox: a random number made for this install (the inbox keeps only a
          hash of it), its version, your system and which sites you've linked. That's how Ravenpost's users are counted,
          nothing about you or your characters.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every six hours</strong>, it asks GitHub whether a newer version is out. Nothing of yours goes with the
          question.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>It never</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>sends any file but these.</li>
        <li>keeps your Battle.net account. The database stores only a hash of it.</li>
        <li>sends your chat, or a line naming another player.</li>
        <li>puts your name, class or race in what you share with the database.</li>
        <li>
          reads any other addon's saved files. Of the rest of the game folder, it only looks at which of these addons are
          installed, and which accounts and characters have saved files.
        </li>
        <li>runs what an addon saved. Saved files are read as data, never executed.</li>
        <li>touches the game itself, injects anything into it or reads its memory.</li>
        <li>updates itself. When a new version is out, it tells you, and you download it.</li>
        <li>shows system notifications. What it sent is in its own window.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can leave out a character or a whole game account in its window. Its Settings page has a Diagnostics text to
        paste into an issue: it names no character, no account and holds no token.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>
